"Causal Edit Serialization (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22820v1)\nciteturn41236view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22820v1\",\"lineno\":292}); Total lines: 1228\nL240: ### 4.3 Pretraining Comparison and Representation Cost\nL241: \nL242: Figure cite121†2 compares pretrained models and representation costs. The controlled suite is balanced across insertion, deletion, replacement, movement, and mixed repair, with 1, 2, 4, or 8 operations. The eight-operation stratum is a denser stress condition. These tests measure recovery of known targets, not whether a reference is the only acceptable revision of natural text.\nL243: #### Language modeling and general capabilities (A/B).\nL244: \nL245: Clean AR has lower validation bits per byte (BPB) than from-scratch CES at all three depths, while CORE differences vary with depth. BPB measures ordinary next-token prediction on clean ClimbMix text (lower is better); the centered CORE aggregate (cite122†Li et al., 2025 ) measures sampled language-understanding and knowledge-task performance. At depth 24, CES obtains 0.7629 BPB versus 0.7484 for clean AR, a relative increase of approximately 1.9%.\nL246: #### Complete repair (C/D).\nL247: \nL248: CES usually produces executable programs, but exact target recovery remains difficult: depth-24 pretrained CES achieves $90.53\\pm 1.85$% valid programs and $5.27\\pm 0.45$% exact recovery. AR-DSL and flat-relative addressing produce no valid controlled programs in this setting. This supports the combined CES addressing design under the evaluated recipe; it does not isolate the pointer head from dual-index RoPE or establish that textual patches generally fail.\nL249: #### Partial repair (E/F).\nL250: \nL251: CES brings more inputs closer to the target than iterative Levenshtein at depth 24 ($53.40\\pm 3.75$% versus $39.03\\pm 0.21$%), but its mean similarity gain is smaller ($0.00941\\pm 0.00147$ versus $0.01062\\pm 0.00046$). Similarity change is\nL252: \nL253:  | $$\\Delta S=S(\\hat{y},y^{*})-S(x,y^{*}),$$  |\nL254: where $S$ is Python’s difflib.SequenceMatcher similarity ratio, based on matching character blocks (autojunk=False). Panel E reports $\\Pr(\\Delta S>0)$; F reports $\\mathbb{E}[\\Delta S]$, retaining both helpful and harmful changes. These target-recovery metrics do not measure the semantic quality of unrestricted revision.\nL255: Across the three tested depths, CES improves in clean-language modeling, CORE, program validity, and both the frequency and mean magnitude of partial repair; exact recovery also rises, but remains low at 5.27% at depth 24.\nL256: #### Localization and payload generation.\nL257: Supplying edit locations does not solve content generation. Oracle FIM still produces harmful payloads, and a separate seed-42 CES diagnostic obtains only 7.3% depth-24 exact recovery with gold pointers and 8.4% with the gold operation envelope as well (Table cite123†6 in Appendix cite82†G ). Predicting locations remains difficult: predicted-span FIM achieves exact span accuracies of 4.67%, 3.93%, and 2.37% at the three depths, although its poor BPB also indicates a weak trained comparator.\nL258: In a matched single-seed depth-12 ablation, sharing one pointer token across roles yielded similar language-modeling and editing scores, while synthetic pointer accuracy fell from 48.64% to 45.81% (Appendix cite99†K ).\nL259: #### Pretrained adaptation.\nL260: \nL261: Adapted CES retains much of its initial clean-language performance, but its short adaptation schedule gives weak repair. It establishes compatibility with pretrained weights, not a compute-matched advantage of adaptation.\nL262: #### Representation cost and preservation (G).\nL263: CES preserves text outside its selected operations and represents sparse revisions without regenerating unchanged text. On CoEdIT pairs (cite124†Raheja et al., 2023 ), minimum insert/delete/replace completions average 41.8 tokens for CES versus 56.0 for whole-target regeneration; including prompts gives approximately 100.0 versus 132.1 tokens. These reference-based costs assume fixed prompts and BPE boundaries; compactness does not establish edit quality or a quality-matched speedup.\nL264: Terminal editing requires no cache rebuild, and generation can continue from the appended source-plus-program history. Continuing instead from materialized edited text requires cache recomputation; its cost depends on context length and rebuild frequency. Full sequence accounting and measured editing and continuation costs are given in Appendix cite86†I .\nL265: Appendix cite82†G evaluates supervised CoEdIT fine-tuning using reference-distance metrics and ERRANT (the grammatical Error Annotation Toolkit) (cite125†Bryant et al., 2017 ), which compares predicted edits with reference edits to compute precision, recall, and $F_{0.5}$.\nL266: After supervised fine-tuning, regeneration scores higher on reference-based GEC metrics while CES retains positive controlled repair, motivating targeted post-training; the separate judged evaluation below does not establish a reversal of this baseline ranking.\nL267: ### 4.4 Post-Training: Judged Grammatical Correction\nL268: \nL269: CoEdIT pairs source sentences with grammatical corrections (cite124†Raheja et al., 2023 ), but acceptable revisions can differ from the reference. The selected d24 seed-42 checkpoint is therefore assessed by reference-blind GPT-5 Mini judging after the curriculum in Section cite19†3.7 . Figure cite126†3 compares unfiltered decoding and a conservative acceptance policy, reporting improvement, harm, coverage, and nominal-clean preservation together.\nL270: Figure 3: CES correction with and without post-generation filtering. The same d24 checkpoint and 5,000 CoEdIT inputs, plus 5,000 nominal-clean counterparts, underlie both evaluations. A–C: unfiltered threshold sweep; dashed lines show independently decoded plain greedy and the dotted line marks threshold 0.5. B shows judged counts; C shows all-input improvement minus worsening. All 2,983 distinct changed outputs have reference-blind GPT-5 Mini judgments.\nL271: D–E: conservative acceptance policy at threshold 0.5. D conditions on 880 retained changes; E uses all-input denominators and includes the human-reference control. CES and human-reference coverage in E are 17.6% and 100%. Intervals are 95% example bootstraps. Separate judge runs make the filtered/unfiltered contrast descriptive. F: manually selected plain-greedy examples without a gate or post-generation filters (5 improved, 3 neutral, 4 worsened).\nL272: Red strike-through marks deletions and green bold marks insertions; indented continuation lines belong to the same example. Category counts are illustrative, not representative frequencies.\nL273: #### Evaluation and decoding policy.\nL274: A custom 5,000-pair CoEdIT confirmation partition is used, with its recorded targets supplied as 5,000 nominal-clean identity inputs. The judge sees the source and anonymous model/human candidates, without identifying the human edit as an answer key. Appendix cite93†J describes the partition and source-overlap checks.\nL275: An optional threshold $\\tau$ controls whether editing begins: unlike plain greedy, the gate combines probabilities across edit openers, so $\\tau=0$ is not equivalent to greedy decoding (Appendix cite90†J ).\nL276: #### Unfiltered edits and selectivity.\nL277: Plain greedy yields positive net judged improvement of $+4.74$ percentage points (95% CI [3.52,5.96]), changing 22.74% of inputs: 619 improve, 136 are neutral and 382 worsen. It changes 3.48% of nominal-clean inputs. More aggressive editing increases harm: at $\\tau=-1$, coverage reaches 59.62% but net improvement falls to $-4.38$ points. At $\\tau=0.75$, net improvement is $+5.18$ points with 19.66% coverage; this threshold is exploratory rather than independently validated.\nL278: Appendix cite98†J.2 gives decoding limits and replay checks; Figure cite126†3 F illustrates successes, neutral changes, and failures.\nL279: #### Conservative acceptance policy.\nL280: Filtering yields more selective positive results. At $\\tau=0.5$, candidates are accepted only if they change at most four removed-plus-inserted characters across at most one region and have mean program-decision log probability at least $-0.6$; rejections return the source. At 17.60% coverage, 68.30% of retained changes improve and 15.68% worsen, giving all-input net improvement of $+9.26$ points. Only 1.48% of nominal-clean inputs change.\nL281: The human control has 100% changed coverage and $+61.94$ points, so it is not coverage-matched. Together, the unfiltered and filtered results establish positive judged editing capability in a CES model, but do not isolate the contribution of RL or establish superiority to general GEC systems; full-curriculum/filter-matched controls and independent human judging remain absent.\nL282: ### 4.5 Continuation After Edit Programs\nL283: CES can also condition subsequent predictions on an appended edit program while retaining the original source history. On 540 held-out ClimbMix documents, a source prefix is corrupted and a correct repair program is supplied. The next 128 tokens of the original document are then scored under teacher forcing. Relative to the same corrupted prefix followed by an empty program, the repair lowers mean negative log likelihood (NLL) by 0.02123 nats/token.\nL284: The reduction is 0.00641 nats/token relative to a valid sham program with the same operation family and span size that leaves the prefix incorrect. Thus the supplied correction makes the original continuation more likely, providing evidence that the model uses the repair content. This tests interpretation of supplied repairs, not autonomous repair or sampled continuation quality.\nL285: Appendix cite84†H gives the protocol and uncertainty; Appendix cite86†I reports cache-rebuilding costs when continuing from materialized edited text.\nL286: ## 5 Conclusion\nL287: \nL288: CES integrates explicit source addressing and edit programs into a causal decoder. The experiments establish controlled repair, beneficial post-trained edits under model judging, and continuation conditioned on supplied repairs. These results support causal referencing as a practical modeling interface; reliable unrestricted revision and transfer to larger pretrained models remain open.\nL289: \nL290: ## 6 Limitations and Future Work\nL291: #### Coverage and generalization.\nL292: \nL293: Experiments use one NanoChat model family up to 1.389B parameters and a 2,048-token context. Exact repair remains low, dense edits are difficult, and insertion recovery is a persistent weakness. Generated training edits are replacement-heavy and copy/move coverage is uneven. The trained regeneration, FIM, and iterative baselines do not represent state-of-the-art editing systems. Transfer to modern LLM families remains untested.\nL294: #### Post-training and measurement.\nL295: The selected confirmation policy permits only small local changes and abstains on 82.4% of editing inputs. Its positive semantic direction is specific to a GPT-5 Mini judge, candidate panel, custom holdout, and one post-training seed. The same model family generated some supervision and judged the outputs. Changing the set of candidates presented to the judge changed scores for identical CES outputs, and pair-based splitting allowed 24 repeated sources.\nL296: Example bootstraps do not capture training or judge uncertainty. There is no independent blinded human audit or confirmation-set comparison with continued SFT or regeneration matched for the full training curriculum and acceptance filters.\nL297: #### Systems and next steps.\nL298: \nL299: Appending programs preserves caches but does not reclaim context; materializing edited text requires rebuilding. Timings depend on hardware, kernels, and context. Source-grouped splits, coverage-matched comparisons with stronger editors, and broader copy/move and long-context evaluations are needed.\nL300: #### Future work.\nL301: A shared pointer token could support more general source references whose roles are specified through instructions or in-context examples, extending the ablation in Appendix cite99†K . Improved initialization and adaptation training for the localization head and dual-index attention could help integrate these mechanisms into pretrained LLMs. Joint training on programs and pointers could teach models to generate code that reads referenced spans and writes computed results back to selected locations.\nL302: Better teacher repairs and task distributions could develop strong referencing with less corruption training. Equal-FLOP comparisons and scaling studies could examine how model capacity and corruption exposure affect editing ability and ordinary autoregressive performance. Larger models and practical text, code, and agent-context tasks would test these capabilities beyond controlled repair.\nL303: References could remain unexpanded until the model requests materialization of a region for editing, with addresses remapped to the expanded content. This makes cache reuse, faster partial rebuilding, and approximate KV-cache updates important systems directions. An edit-based discrete diffusion model is another possibility: training across noise levels could support generation from random text through repeatedly generated and applied edit programs, with the aim of approximating the training distribution.\nL304: ## Impact Statement\nL305: \nL306: Explicit edit programs can make text and code revision easier to inspect. They can also support subtle document manipulation or introduce harmful changes into otherwise correct text. Structural validity and model confidence are insufficient safeguards for semantic correctness. The current models are research artifacts; downstream applications should expose proposed changes for review and preserve provenance. Generated data and derivatives retain applicable upstream restrictions.\nL307: ## Software and Data\nL308: \nL309: CES extends NanoChat (cite120†Karpathy, 2025 ). Code is available at cite127†https://github.com/Louiii/CES†github.com ; data, tokenizer, and model checkpoints are available at cite128†https://huggingface.co/datasets/lorob/CES†huggingface.co .\nL310: \nL311: ## Acknowledgements and Disclosures\nL312: \nL313: #### Funding.\nL314: \nL315: This research received no external funding and was conducted independently of the author’s employment.\nL316: #### Competing interests.\nL317: \nL318: The author is employed by InstaDeep. This work was conducted independently and does not represent the views of InstaDeep.\nL319: ## References\nL320:   * Ahmadian et al. [2024] Arash Ahmadian, Chris Cremer, Matthias Gallé, Marzieh Fadaee, Julia Kreutzer, Olivier Pietquin, Ahmet Üstün, and Sara Hooker. Back to basics: Revisiting reinforce-style optimization for learning from human feedback in llms. In Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), 2024. URL cite129†https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.662/†aclanthology.org .\nL321:   * Bavarian et al. [2022] Mohammad Bavarian, Heewoo Jun, Nikolas Tezak, John Schulman, Christine McLeavey, Jerry Tworek, and Mark Chen. Efficient training of language models to fill in the middle, 2022. URL cite130†https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.14255 .\nL322:   * Brown et al. [2020] Tom B. Brown, Benjamin Mann, Nick Ryder, Melanie Subbiah, Jared Kaplan, Prafulla Dhariwal, Arvind Neelakantan, Pranav Shyam, Girish Sastry, Amanda Askell, Sandhini Agarwal, Ariel Herbert-Voss, Gretchen Krueger, Tom Henighan, Rewon Child, Aditya Ramesh, Daniel M. Ziegler, Jeffrey Wu, Clemens Winter, Christopher Hesse, Mark Chen, Eric Sigler, Mateusz Litwin, Scott Gray, Benjamin Chess, Jack Clark, Christopher Berner, Sam 