ps://arxiv.org/abs/1910.10683 .\nL337:   * Raheja et al. [2023] Vipul Raheja, Dhruv Kumar, Ryan Koo, and Dongyeop Kang. CoEdIT: Text editing by task-specific instruction tuning. In Houda Bouamor, Juan Pino, and Kalika Bali, editors, Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2023, pages 5274–5291, Singapore, December 2023. Association for Computational Linguistics. doi: 10.18653/v1/2023.findings-emnlp.350. URL cite142†https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-emnlp.350/†aclanthology.org .\nL338:   * Reid and Neubig [2022] Machel Reid and Graham Neubig. Learning to model editing processes. arXiv preprint arXiv:2205.12374, 2022.\nL339:   * Song et al. [2019] Kaitao Song, Xu Tan, Tao Qin, Jianfeng Lu, and Tie-Yan Liu. Mass: Masked sequence to sequence pre-training for language generation, 2019. URL cite143†https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.02450 .\nL340:   * Stern et al. [2019] Mitchell Stern, William Chan, Jamie Kiros, and Jakob Uszkoreit. Insertion transformer: Flexible sequence generation via insertion operations, 2019. URL cite144†https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.03249 .\nL341:   * Su et al. [2023] Jianlin Su, Yu Lu, Shengfeng Pan, Ahmed Murtadha, Bo Wen, and Yunfeng Liu. Roformer: Enhanced transformer with rotary position embedding, 2023. URL cite145†https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.09864 .\nL342:   * Vaswani et al. [2017] Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, Jakob Uszkoreit, Llion Jones, Aidan N Gomez, Łukasz Kaiser, and Illia Polosukhin. Attention is all you need. Advances in neural information processing systems, 30, 2017.\nL343:   * Vinyals et al. [2015] Oriol Vinyals, Meire Fortunato, and Navdeep Jaitly. Pointer networks. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, volume 28, 2015. URL cite146†https://papers.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/29921001f2f04bd3baee84a12e98098f-Abstract.html†papers.neurips.cc .\nL344: ## Appendix A Reproducibility, Protocols, and Asset Provenance\nL345: \nL346: The comparison contains eight editing methods at three depths and three training seeds; each is evaluated before and after supervised CoEdIT fine-tuning. Nine clean AR checkpoints are additionally reported as language-model references.\nL347: ### A.1 Training budgets and implementation\nL348: \nL349: Table cite147†2 gives training budgets and optimization settings.\nL350: Setting  | d12  | d18  | d24\nL351: CES parameters (millions)  | 287.44  | 704.55  | 1,388.84\nL352: Causal pretraining updates  | 26,880  | 39,600  | 74,800\nL353: Serialized token slots (millions)  | 880.80  | 1,297.61  | 2,451.05\nL354: Iterative editor updates  | 8,960  | 13,200  | 24,933\nL355: Clean AR reference updates  | 26,880  | 39,600  | 4,675\nL356: Additional CES adaptation updates  | 1,000  | 1,000  | 1,000\nL357: Supervised causal GEC updates  | 150  | 150  | 150\nL358: Iterative GEC updates  | 50  | 50  | 50\nL359: Pretraining stage  | Slots/update  | LR warmup  | LR decay fraction  | Muon WD schedule\nL360: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL361: Six causal editing methods  | 32,768  | 40  | 0.20  | Constant\nL362: Additional CES adaptation  | 32,768  | 40  | 0.20  | Constant^{∗}\nL363: Iterative Levenshtein  | 32,768  | 40  | 0.20  | Constant, unscaled\nL364: Table 2: Training budgets and optimization settings for the method comparison. Context length is 2,048. Nominal token-slot budgets and clean-token exposure are distinct quantities. Warmup is in updates; LR decay occupies the final stated fraction and ends at 0.05 of the peak rate. ^{∗}Adaptation weight decay is disabled during its initial frozen-backbone stage. Iterative Levenshtein uses three encoder passes per step; pass-count matching approximates computation, not equal FLOPs or wall-clock cost.\nL365: Training uses Muon for matrix parameters and AdamW for embeddings, unembedding, and scalar parameters. Editing-method pretraining uses H100 tensorwise FP8 training and evaluation in the base compute dtype. Model/data seed pairs are $(42,1234)$, $(43,1235)$, and $(44,1236)$ for the causal methods; the iterative trainer uses seeds 42/43/44 for both initialization and data generation. The final position-loss weight is 0.01 for CES, adapted CES, and predicted FIM.\nL366: #### Shared causal pretraining settings.\nL367: \nL368: The raw matrix/embedding/unembedding/scalar learning-rate arguments for the causal grid are 0.02/0.3/0.008/0.5; the trainer applies batch and width scaling. The nominal Muon weight decay is 0.28, scaled by batch size and model parameter counts.\nL369: #### Iterative training.\nL370: \nL371: The iterative trainer applies the same batch and width learning-rate scaling, but its Muon weight decay remains 0.28 without the causal trainer’s scaling. Both trainers ramp Muon momentum from 0.85 to 0.97 over 400 updates; only the causal trainer decreases it to 0.90 during the final LR decay.\nL372: #### Supervised CoEdIT fine-tuning.\nL373: All eight editing methods are separately fine-tuned on the 12,756 CoEdIT source–target training pairs, without mixing in clean-text replay. This supervised comparison is distinct from the balanced SFT and RL curriculum in Appendix cite90†J . The seven causal methods use 16,384 token slots per update, raw matrix/embedding/unembedding/scalar learning rates 0.002/0.03/0.0008/0.05, a nominal constant Muon weight decay of 0.05, 20 warmup steps, a 0.30 warmdown fraction, and a final learning-rate fraction of 0.10.\nL374: The same batch/width LR and Muon decay scaling are applied. The iterative trainer uses 50 three-pass updates rather than 150 causal updates, 10 warmup steps, and unscaled Muon weight decay 0.05.\nL375: #### Pretrained-decoder adaptation.\nL376: \nL377: Of its 1,000 additional updates, the first 250 train only new components; imported parameters then use a 0.1 learning-rate multiplier. The additional adaptation budget and separate clean pretraining prevent an equal-budget comparison with from-scratch CES. Architecture and initialization are specified in Appendix cite71†F .\nL378: ### A.2 Metric and evaluation contracts\nL379: \nL380: Controlled repair uses 1,000 examples before fine-tuning and a 250-example stratified subset afterward; method-grid GEC metrics use the 485 official CoEdIT validation examples. CES pointer accuracy, textual-coordinate accuracy, and exact FIM span accuracy measure different decisions and are not compared as a shared metric.\nL381: #### Clean-language evaluation.\nL382: \nL383: BPB uses 1,048,576 validation-token positions, sequence length 2,048, and batches of eight. CORE uses a fixed selection of up to 50 examples per task with few-shot contexts. Clean AR is evaluated only on these language-model metrics.\nL384: #### Editing metrics.\nL385: \nL386: Character similarity uses SequenceMatcher, including for iterative Levenshtein; GEC closer/farther rates use token Levenshtein distance, and semantic improvement/harm uses the stated model judge. Invalid and rejected outputs resolve to the source in unconditional metrics. Table cite123†6 reports oracle CES diagnostics; Appendix cite99†K reports shared-pointer accuracy.\nL387: ### A.3 Compute resources\nL388: At depth 24, pretraining on one H100 (80GB) takes approximately 8 hours for CES, AR-DSL, regeneration, and predicted-span FIM; 9–10 hours for flat-relative and oracle-span FIM; 6.7 hours for adapted CES (including clean pretraining); and 10.6 hours for iterative Levenshtein. For d24 CES on two RTX 5080s (16GB each), the 800-update balanced SFT stage takes approximately 21 minutes, followed by 33 minutes of broad RL (100 updates) and 41 minutes of GEC RL (200 updates).\nL389: These are recorded training-loop times, excluding setup, checkpoint I/O, and evaluation.\nL390: The shared-pointer ablation uses RTX 5080s. The cache-rebuilding benchmark uses an RTX 5080; editing-latency comparisons use an H100 (Appendix cite86†I ). GPT-5 Mini pretraining-edit generation used approximately 0.5B API tokens and $200 in credits, excluding GEC corruption filtering, semantic judging, and post-training experiments.\nL391: \nL392: Main confirmation judging cost approximately $5; auxiliary judging and corruption-filtering experiments cost approximately $40, estimated from recorded GPT-5 Mini API usage.\nL393: Recorded method-comparison training logs account for approximately 260 H100 GPU-hours and 34 RTX 5080 GPU-hours. These totals cover recorded completed training segments, excluding the post-training stages above, evaluation, and additional preliminary or unsuccessful experiments. The unfiltered confirmation inference on 5,000 editing and 5,000 clean inputs takes approximately 10 minutes on one RTX 5080, including the plain-greedy and forced-edit decodes.\nL394: ## Appendix B Corruption Distribution and Data Quality\nL395: \nL396: Self-supervised training pairs are generated by sampling a stochastic stream of insertions, deletions, replacements, and moves under a fixed serialization budget. The process is parameterized by edit-spacing and edit-length distributions, an insertion-content mixture, and optional protection of a clean prefix as stable context.\nL397: #### Edit arrival and lengths.\nL398: \nL399: Distances to candidate deletion and insertion events follow geometric distributions, with per-token start probabilities $p^{\\text{del}}_{\\text{start}}$ and $p^{\\text{ins}}_{\\text{start}}$:\nL400: \nL401:  | $$\\Delta^{\\text{del}}\\sim\\mathrm{Geom}(p^{\\text{del}}_{\\text{start}}),\\qquad\\Delta^{\\text{ins}}\\sim\\mathrm{Geom}(p^{\\text{ins}}_{\\text{start}}).$$  |\nL402: \nL403: The earlier event determines the next operation. Edit lengths follow\nL404:  | $$L^{\\text{del}}\\sim\\mathrm{Geom}(p^{\\text{del}}_{\\text{end}}),\\qquad L^{\\text{ins}}\\sim\\mathrm{Geom}(p^{\\text{ins}}_{\\text{end}}),$$  |\nL405: \nL406: where $\\mathrm{Geom}(p)$ returns a positive integer with mean $1/p$. In practice these are parameterized via means $(\\mu^{\\text{del}}_{\\text{gap}},\\mu^{\\text{del}}_{\\text{len}},\\mu^{\\text{ins}}_{\\text{gap}},\\mu^{\\text{ins}}_{\\text{len}})$ by setting $p=1/\\mu$.\nL407: #### Token-budgeted serialization.\nL408: \nL409: Sampling stops when the next edit would exceed the serialized token budget; that edit is dropped. Operation counts vary within the fixed maximum length.\nL410: #### Insert-content source mixture.\nL411: \nL412: For each insertion-style payload of length $L^{\\text{ins}}$, inserted tokens are drawn from a mixture distribution over sources:\nL413: \nL414:  | $$s\\sim\\mathrm{Categorical}(\\pi_{\\text{buf}},\\pi_{\\text{self}},\\pi_{\\text{uni}},\\pi_{\\text{marg}}),$$  |\nL415: The sources are a replay reservoir (Buffer), a span from the current sequence (Self), uniform random tokens (Uniform), and a marginal unigram distribution (Marginal). Reported random-corruption runs use weights 0.6/0.1/0.2/0.1, renormalized over available sources, with uniform sampling as fallback when no non-uniform source is available.\nL416: #### Protecting a prefix as stable context.\nL417: \nL418: A sampled protection length $P$ restricts edits to the final $N-P$ tokens, leaving the first $P$ tokens as uncorrupted conditioning context.\nL419: #### Source text and generated edits.\nL420: ClimbMix is the 400B-token pretraining mixture associated with Nemotron-CLIMB [cite118†Diao et al., 2025 ]. An approximately 15GB shard subset of NanoChat’s shuffled release, karpathy/climbmix-400b-shuffle, is used. The 2.5% sample used for GPT-5 Mini edit generation is drawn from this subset, not the full 400B-token corpus. The bootstrap reserves shard_06542.parquet for validation; the source corpus is distributed under CC BY-NC 4.0.\nL421: GPT-5 Mini proposes localized edits, which are parsed into the CES language. These are synthetic revisions, not human annotations. GPT-5 Mini emits one operation per line in a text-based domain-specific language: Del(region), Replace(region, text), Insert(pointer, text), or Move(region, pointer). A RegionInclusive identifies a source span using exact search phrases for its start and end, with start_exact and end_exact substrings marking the inclusive boundaries within those phrases.\nL422: A Pointer identifies an insertion boundary using a source search phrase and adjacent pre and post strings: insertion occurs between them. For example, pre=\"chicken, \" and post=\"seafood\" identify the boundary in chicken, seafood. All anchors refer to the unmodified source; only the text payload may introduce new content. During data preparation, these text anchors are resolved against the source and converted to token-coordinate edits for CES serialization.\n"
